<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Benjamin Kállay</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1839">1839</a>–<a href="/years/1903">1903</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Béni Kállay</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Benjamin Kállay was a Hungarian statesman and historian who served as joint finance minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> from 1882 to 1903, in charge of the administration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herzegovina">Herzegovina</a>. He promoted a shared Bosnian identity to counter Serbian and Croatian nationalism and developed railways, mines, forestry and schools. His rule left a strong mark on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarajevo">Sarajevo</a>'s architecture and institutions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/benjamin-kallay.jpg" alt="Benjamin Kállay" width="320" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ede Ellinger · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Herr_von_K%C3%A1llay_(Minister_of_Finance,_Austria_Hungary)_1899_Ede_Ellinger.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
